course: adult-life
lesson: 21
title: Hospitals, operations and serious illness
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When someone in the family is ill or hurt, grown-ups use special hospital words that can sound frightening, but most of them have plain meanings.
explain: A&E is the emergency part of a hospital, an operation is a surgeon fixing something inside the body, and "put under" means a deep, safe sleep so nothing hurts.
explain: Picture a kitchen in Manchester: Mum yawns over her toast and says, "We had to take Grandad to A&E last night." He had a pain in his chest; the doctors checked him, and he is staying in for a few days.
explain: Some illnesses, like cancer, are serious and take months of treatment, and some checks, like scans and tests, are only there to be careful.
explain: Waiting is a big part of hospitals: waiting to be seen, waiting for results, waiting for visiting hours. Grown-ups often feel worried and short-tempered while they wait.
fact: A&E means Accident and Emergency, the hospital department for sudden, serious illness and injuries, open all day and all night.
fact: In A&E a nurse checks everyone first and the most serious cases go in first, not the people who came first, so a sprained wrist can wait hours.
fact: An operation, or surgery, is when a surgeon fixes or removes something inside the body; afterwards the person needs rest and must not lift heavy things.
fact: "Put him under" means giving a general anaesthetic, a medicine that makes you sleep deeply so you feel no pain; a specialist doctor watches you the whole time.
fact: A local anaesthetic only numbs one part of the body, and the person stays awake.
fact: Hospitals limit visiting hours and the number of visitors so patients can rest and doctors and nurses can work.
fact: Cancer is when some cells in the body grow out of control and form lumps that can spread; you cannot catch it from someone.
fact: Chemo, short for chemotherapy, is strong medicine that kills cancer cells; it can make people very tired and sick for months, and many people recover.
fact: Some cancer treatments make hair fall out because they attack fast-growing cells; the hair usually grows back after treatment.
fact: Blood pressure is how hard the blood pushes on the walls of the blood vessels; high blood pressure has no symptoms but strains the heart over years.
fact: Tablets, less salt and more exercise can lower high blood pressure, and measuring it only feels like a tight squeeze.
fact: Results of blood tests and scans can take days or weeks, and waiting for them is often the most worrying part; most results come back fine.
fact: A scan looks inside the body; "just to be on the safe side" means the doctor is being careful, and most scans find nothing serious.
exercise: Mum says, "We had to take Grandad to A&E last night." What is A&E? | The hospital department for sudden, serious illness and injuries | The part of the hospital where babies are born and new mums rest afterwards | A shop inside the hospital that sells medicine | The office where you book a visit to the GP
exercise: Why might a boy with a hurt wrist wait four hours in A&E while others go in before him? | The most serious cases are seen first, not the earliest | The nurses forgot that he was sitting in the room with his mum | People who live closest to the hospital go first | Children always have to wait until all adults are seen
exercise: "Auntie is going in for an operation on Tuesday." What will happen to her? | A surgeon will fix or remove something inside her body | A nurse will give her a bandage and send her home with some tablets | She will start a new job at the hospital | She will have her eyes tested for new glasses
exercise: After her operation, what will Auntie most likely need? | Rest for a while and no heavy lifting | To go straight back to the gym that evening | A big party with lots of guests at home | Nothing at all, as if it never happened
exercise: "They'll put him under. He won't feel a thing." What does "put under" mean? | Given a medicine that makes him sleep deeply during the operation | Buried under the ground after he has died | Put in a lower bed so that he cannot fall out of it during the night | Told to keep quiet and not complain about pain
exercise: What is the difference between a general and a local anaesthetic? | A local one numbs one part and you stay awake | A local one is only used in your own town | A general one is weaker and only numbs your skin | There is no difference, they are two names for one thing
exercise: A sign says "Visiting hours 2 till 4. Two visitors per bed." Why do hospitals have rules like this? | So patients can rest and staff can do their work | So the hospital can charge money for each visit made to a patient | Because visitors might catch every illness on the ward | Because doctors do not like meeting families at all
exercise: Mum says gently, "Mrs Patel has cancer and she's having chemo." What is chemo? | Strong medicine that kills cancer cells | A special diet of fruit that cures cancer in a few weeks | An operation to take out the whole stomach | A machine that checks your blood pressure
exercise: Can you catch cancer by visiting Mrs Patel? | No, cancer is not catching | Yes, if you stand close to her | Only if you share a drink with her | Only if you touch her headscarf
exercise: Why might a grown-up having cancer treatment have no hair? | The treatment attacks fast-growing cells, and hair cells grow fast | She shaved it because she was far too hot in her bed in the hospital | Cancer always makes hair fall out before treatment starts | The doctors cut it off so they can see her head
exercise: You see a lady with no hair in a soft headscarf. What is the kindest thing to do? | Talk to her about normal things and try not to stare | Ask her loudly why she has no hair | Keep away from her in case it spreads to you or your family | Tell her she would look better in a wig
exercise: The nurse says, "Your blood pressure's a bit high." What is blood pressure? | How hard the blood pushes on the walls of the blood vessels | How much blood is inside your body altogether, counted in litres | How fast you can breathe after running | How heavy your blood is compared with water
exercise: Grandpa feels fine. Why is his high blood pressure still worth treating? | Over years it strains the heart, even with no symptoms | It will make him feel dizzy every single minute of the day | It means he will need an operation tomorrow | It is catching and his family could get it too
exercise: Mum says, "I'm waiting for my test results." What is true about test results? | They can take days or weeks, and most come back fine | They always arrive the same afternoon as the test is done | They are only ever sent when something is wrong | Waiting for them means the person is surely very ill
exercise: Mum says, "I'm going for a scan, just to be on the safe side." What does this mean? | The doctor is being careful, and it is probably nothing serious | The doctor is sure she is very ill and needs surgery straight away | She has to go and have her photo taken for a new passport | The hospital is checking that its building is safe
exercise: Why is Mum a bit snappy this week while she waits for results? | Waiting is worrying, so grown-ups get short-tempered | She is cross because you did something wrong at school this week | Hospitals make people angry on purpose | She already knows the results are very bad
test: You overhear Dad on the phone: "They kept him in A&E all night, chest pains, but he's stable now." What happened to Grandad? | He went to the emergency department and is now steady | He went to the dentist and had a big tooth taken out | He was told off at work and had to stay very late | He went to a care home to live there for good with other old people
test: Your brother grumbles, "A&E's well unfair, we were there first and that bloke went in before us." How do you put him right? | The nurse sends the most serious cases in first | Grown-ups always go in before children | People who shout the loudest go in first | The man must have paid extra to skip the queue
test: Gran says, "Bert's having his knee done under a local, so he'll be awake for it." Which is true? | Only his knee will be numb, and he will stay awake | He will be asleep the whole time and feel nothing at all | He will have the operation in his own house | He will feel all the pain because nothing is used
test: Cousin Jade says, "Don't hug Mrs Patel, you'll catch her cancer." Is Jade right? | No, cancer cannot be caught from another person | Yes, hugging is the main way that cancer spreads from person to person | Only children can catch it, so she is right | Only if Mrs Patel has lost her hair
test: Two nurses chat by the ward: "Chemo's knocked her for six, poor love, but her bloods are looking good." What does this mean? | The treatment makes her very tired, but her tests look hopeful | She was hit by a cricket ball in the ward | She has stopped her treatment because she is cured | The chemo has failed and nothing more can be done for her by anyone
test: A neighbour gossips, "He's lost all his hair, must be on his last legs." What is wrong with this? | Hair loss comes from treatment and the hair usually grows back | Nothing, losing hair always means someone is dying very soon | Hair loss only happens to people who are very old | People only lose hair when they stop eating
test: Grandpa jokes, "Doc says my pressure's through the roof, but I feel grand, so whatever." Why should he still listen? | High blood pressure harms the heart over years without warning signs | High blood pressure will make him feel ill very soon | His roof needs mending before winter comes | Feeling grand proves the doctor's machine is broken
test: Mum hisses at Dad, "Not in front of the kids, I don't get the results till Thursday." What is she waiting for? | The answers from her tests, which take time to come back | Marks from a school exam she took last week at evening classes | A parcel that the hospital is posting to her | The lottery numbers drawn on Thursday night
test: Your sister says, "Mum's having a scan, so she must be really, really sick." How do you put her right? | A scan is often just a careful check, and most find nothing | A scan is only given to people who are dying and cannot be helped | Scans are a kind of operation that cuts you open | Mum is fine because scans are only for babies
test: On the news: "Hospitals warn of long waits in emergency departments this winter." Why are waits longest then? | Winter brings more illness, so A&E is busier | Doctors take their summer holidays in winter | Hospitals close half their beds every December | Patients choose to come in winter for fun
test: Dad mutters, "Two visitors a bed, so you lot take turns, all right?" Why must you take turns? | The ward allows only two visitors at each bed | Children are never allowed on any hospital ward | Grandad only wants to see one person a day | The car park is too small for the whole family
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the order in which patients are attended to in an Accident and Emergency department? | Patients are seen in order of clinical urgency, not order of arrival | Patients are seen strictly in the order in which they arrived | Patients of advanced age are invariably seen before all others | Patients residing nearest the hospital are accorded priority
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the administration of a general anaesthetic prior to a surgical procedure. | To induce a deep sleep such that the patient experiences no pain | To ensure that the patient remains fully awake and alert throughout | To remove the necessity for any subsequent period of rest | To cure the underlying illness without recourse to surgery
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning chemotherapy and the loss of hair. | The treatment affects rapidly growing cells, and hair ordinarily regrows thereafter | Loss of hair signifies that the treatment has wholly failed | Loss of hair is caused by the cancer itself and is permanent | The patient's hair is removed deliberately by hospital staff
exam: It is given that a patient has been referred for a scan "as a precautionary measure". Determine the most accurate interpretation of this phrase. | The physician is exercising caution; a serious finding is not presumed | The physician has already established the presence of a grave illness | The patient is required to undergo immediate surgery | The scan is a routine procedure applied only to infants
